window.OCS_HELP_INDEX = [{"title":"What OpenCAD Studio does","chapter":"Getting Started","url":"getting-started.html#what-opencad-studio-does","text":"What OpenCAD Studio does OpenCAD Studio is a desktop and web CAD application for opening, creating, editing, visualizing, and saving two- and three-dimensional drawings. It works with DWG and DXF drawings and provides command-line, ribbon, automation, and plugin-oriented workflows."},{"title":"A first drawing","chapter":"Getting Started","url":"getting-started.html#a-first-drawing","text":"A first drawing Create a new drawing or open an existing DWG or DXF file. Confirm the active workspace, units, layer, and object-snap settings. Create geometry with a drawing command such as Line , Circle , or Polyline . Edit the result with grips or a modification command. Save the drawing under a new name until its compatibility has been verified. Data safety: Keep an untouched copy of important third-party drawings. Confirm geometry, layouts, external references, and plotted output before replacing the original file."},{"title":"How to use this manual","chapter":"Getting Started","url":"getting-started.html#how-to-use-this-manual","text":"How to use this manual Use the chapter navigation for broad workflows, the search box for a command or concept, and the previous/next links for sequential reading. Command names, system variables, keys, and file extensions appear in monospaced text ."},{"title":"Create and open drawings","chapter":"Files and Drawings","url":"files-drawings.html#create-and-open-drawings","text":"Create and open drawings Use New for a blank document and Open for an existing DWG, DXF, backup, or autosave drawing. After opening a file from another CAD system, inspect its units, layers, blocks, fonts, raster references, layouts, and model extents."},{"title":"Save and Save As","chapter":"Files and Drawings","url":"files-drawings.html#save-and-save-as","text":"Save and Save As Save updates the current drawing. Save As changes its path, format, or version. Prefer Save As when validating interoperability or recovering an older drawing. A successful write does not replace visual inspection of a reopened copy."},{"title":"Recovery and backups","chapter":"Files and Drawings","url":"files-drawings.html#recovery-and-backups","text":"Recovery and backups OpenCAD Studio recognizes drawing backups and autosave-style files where the underlying data is DWG or DXF. If strict loading rejects malformed entities, use the recovery workflow and review what was discarded before continuing work."},{"title":"External resources","chapter":"Files and Drawings","url":"files-drawings.html#external-resources","text":"External resources Raster images, underlays, fonts, materials, and external references may depend on paths outside the drawing. Keep referenced files together when moving a project and verify that relative paths still resolve."},{"title":"Model space and paper space","chapter":"Workspace and Navigation","url":"workspace-navigation.html#model-space-and-paper-space","text":"Model space and paper space Model space is the primary drawing environment. Paper-space layouts arrange views for plotting through layout viewports. Always confirm the active space before drawing or editing."},{"title":"Pan, zoom, and orbit","chapter":"Workspace and Navigation","url":"workspace-navigation.html#pan-zoom-and-orbit","text":"Pan, zoom, and orbit Use pan and zoom to navigate without changing drawing geometry. Use orbit and view controls for three-dimensional inspection. Zoom Extents is useful when the drawing appears empty or geometry is far from the expected location."},{"title":"Multiple model viewports","chapter":"Workspace and Navigation","url":"workspace-navigation.html#multiple-model-viewports","text":"Multiple model viewports Model viewports can show different views of the same document. Grid, snap, camera, and render mode may vary by viewport; confirm which viewport is active before entering coordinates."},{"title":"Selection and object snaps","chapter":"Workspace and Navigation","url":"workspace-navigation.html#selection-and-object-snaps","text":"Selection and object snaps Selection identifies the objects a command will modify. Object snaps target precise geometric locations such as endpoints, midpoints, centers, and intersections. Zoom in and verify the snap marker when precision matters."},{"title":"Basic drawing commands","chapter":"Create and Edit Geometry","url":"create-edit-geometry.html#basic-drawing-commands","text":"Basic drawing commands Create lines, arcs, circles, ellipses, polylines, splines, points, text, hatches, and other supported entities from the ribbon or command line. Follow the active prompt: multi-step commands remain active until they complete or are cancelled."},{"title":"Modify geometry","chapter":"Create and Edit Geometry","url":"create-edit-geometry.html#modify-geometry","text":"Modify geometry Common modification workflows include move, copy, rotate, scale, mirror, offset, trim, extend, fillet, chamfer, stretch, join, explode, and erase. Locked layers can prevent modification."},{"title":"Grips and direct manipulation","chapter":"Create and Edit Geometry","url":"create-edit-geometry.html#grips-and-direct-manipulation","text":"Grips and direct manipulation Select an entity to expose its grips. Dragging a grip previews and then commits a geometric change. Constrained neighboring geometry may update with it."},{"title":"Undo, redo, and OOPS","chapter":"Create and Edit Geometry","url":"create-edit-geometry.html#undo-redo-and-oops","text":"Undo, redo, and OOPS Undo reverses recorded document operations and Redo reapplies them. OOPS restores the most recently erased entities without reversing later commands. Save before a risky sequence and verify that associated dimensions, hatches, groups, and constraints were restored with their geometry."},{"title":"Layers","chapter":"Layers and Properties","url":"layers-properties.html#layers","text":"Layers Layers organize geometry and control visibility, locking, color, line type, line weight, and plot behavior. Set the intended active layer before creating objects. Avoid editing objects on locked layers."},{"title":"Object properties","chapter":"Layers and Properties","url":"layers-properties.html#object-properties","text":"Object properties Properties can be assigned explicitly or inherited ByLayer or ByBlock . Use the Properties surface or commands such as CHPROP to inspect and change selected objects."},{"title":"Styles","chapter":"Layers and Properties","url":"layers-properties.html#styles","text":"Styles Text, dimension, multiline, table, and visual styles centralize presentation. Changing a shared style can update many objects, so inspect affected layouts before saving."},{"title":"Create and insert blocks","chapter":"Blocks and References","url":"blocks-references.html#create-and-insert-blocks","text":"Create and insert blocks A block definition groups reusable drawing content; an INSERT places an instance with its own position, rotation, scale, attributes, and optional array settings. Editing a definition affects every reference to it."},{"title":"Array inserts","chapter":"Blocks and References","url":"blocks-references.html#array-inserts","text":"Array inserts Array inserts place multiple block instances with row and column counts and spacing. Large counts can generate substantial geometry. Validate scaled and rotated arrays when exchanging drawings with another CAD system."},{"title":"Attributes and external references","chapter":"Blocks and References","url":"blocks-references.html#attributes-and-external-references","text":"Attributes and external references Attributes attach editable values to block references. External references and underlays retain links to other files; preserve their paths when sharing or archiving a project."},{"title":"Text and annotation","chapter":"Annotation and Dimensions","url":"annotation-dimensions.html#text-and-annotation","text":"Text and annotation Single-line text, multiline text, leaders, multileaders, tables, tolerances, center marks, and centerlines communicate design intent. Missing fonts can change layout or substitute glyphs, especially in the web build."},{"title":"Dimensions","chapter":"Annotation and Dimensions","url":"annotation-dimensions.html#dimensions","text":"Dimensions Dimensions combine measured geometry with a dimension style. Associative dimensions update when their source geometry changes; non-associative or partially associated dimensions require manual verification."},{"title":"Annotation scale","chapter":"Annotation and Dimensions","url":"annotation-dimensions.html#annotation-scale","text":"Annotation scale Annotative objects can display differently at different viewport scales. Confirm the active annotation scale before creating annotations and inspect all paper-space viewports before plotting."},{"title":"Layouts and viewports","chapter":"Layouts and Plotting","url":"layouts-plotting.html#layouts-and-viewports","text":"Layouts and viewports Layouts represent sheets. Paper-space viewports expose model-space views at controlled scales. Lock finished viewports to avoid accidental view changes."},{"title":"Page setup and plot preview","chapter":"Layouts and Plotting","url":"layouts-plotting.html#page-setup-and-plot-preview","text":"Page setup and plot preview Choose the target device or file format, paper size, orientation, plot area, scale, style behavior, and output quality. Use preview to catch clipping, incorrect line weights, hidden layers, or an unintended plot area."},{"title":"Export checks","chapter":"Layouts and Plotting","url":"layouts-plotting.html#export-checks","text":"Export checks Before distributing a PDF or plotted file, compare it with the drawing at the intended sheet size and verify text, dimensions, raster content, transparency, line types, and viewport scales."},{"title":"Solids and surfaces","chapter":"3D Modeling and Visualization","url":"3d-modeling.html#solids-and-surfaces","text":"Solids and surfaces OpenCAD Studio can create and display solid primitives and modeled features such as extrusions and revolutions. Imported ACIS-backed bodies are tessellated for display while exact body data is retained where supported."},{"title":"Visual styles and lighting","chapter":"3D Modeling and Visualization","url":"3d-modeling.html#visual-styles-and-lighting","text":"Visual styles and lighting Wireframe, shaded, hidden-line, materials, lights, and shadows affect display, not the underlying object geometry. Switch to wireframe when diagnosing missing faces or material problems."},{"title":"Tessellation quality","chapter":"3D Modeling and Visualization","url":"3d-modeling.html#tessellation-quality","text":"Tessellation quality Curves and analytic surfaces are converted into display geometry according to view and tolerance settings. A coarse display does not necessarily mean the stored curve or solid is coarse."},{"title":"Geometric constraints","chapter":"Parametric Constraints","url":"parametric-constraints.html#geometric-constraints","text":"Geometric constraints Constraints capture relationships such as coincident, horizontal, vertical, parallel, perpendicular, equal, tangent, distance, angle, and radius. Use the degree-of-freedom and conflict indicators to understand the sketch state."},{"title":"Editing constrained geometry","chapter":"Parametric Constraints","url":"parametric-constraints.html#editing-constrained-geometry","text":"Editing constrained geometry Moving or grip-editing constrained geometry can move neighboring objects. If a solve fails or becomes redundant, inspect the constraint glyphs and conflict status before deleting geometry."},{"title":"Persistence and history limitations","chapter":"Parametric Constraints","url":"parametric-constraints.html#persistence-and-history-limitations","text":"Persistence and history limitations Constraint data is stored as OpenCAD Studio extension data inside DWG and DXF. Other applications may preserve it without interpreting it. Constraint edits and geometry changes may currently occupy adjacent history entries; verify the result after undo, redo, erase, copy, and reopen operations."},{"title":"Command line","chapter":"Commands and Shortcuts","url":"commands-shortcuts.html#command-line","text":"Command line Enter a command name or alias, then respond to its prompts. Press Escape to cancel the active command and Return to accept an available default. Exact prompt options vary by command and current selection."},{"title":"Discover commands","chapter":"Commands and Shortcuts","url":"commands-shortcuts.html#discover-commands","text":"Discover commands Use command autocomplete and the ribbon to discover available operations. Some commands are one-shot actions; others maintain state across several point, number, keyword, or object-selection inputs."},{"title":"Shortcut conventions","chapter":"Commands and Shortcuts","url":"commands-shortcuts.html#shortcut-conventions","text":"Shortcut conventions Application and operating-system shortcuts can differ between native and web builds. Avoid assigning browser-reserved shortcuts to web-only workflows."},{"title":"Automation interface","chapter":"Automation and Plugins","url":"automation-plugins.html#automation-interface","text":"Automation interface The automation interface can inspect documents, run commands, query geometry, capture the viewport, and perform batches. Callers should use request IDs and document revisions to avoid repeating or applying work to stale state."},{"title":"Plugins","chapter":"Automation and Plugins","url":"automation-plugins.html#plugins","text":"Plugins Native plugins can contribute commands and ribbon surfaces through the plugin host. Install only trusted packages that match the host API, compiler, and CAD model compatibility requirements."},{"title":"Native and web differences","chapter":"Automation and Plugins","url":"automation-plugins.html#native-and-web-differences","text":"Native and web differences The browser build uses in-memory file workflows and cannot load native plugin libraries. Some rendering, font, windowing, and parallel-processing behavior also differs from the desktop build."},{"title":"A drawing opens incorrectly","chapter":"Troubleshooting and Recovery","url":"troubleshooting-recovery.html#a-drawing-opens-incorrectly","text":"A drawing opens incorrectly Check units, extents, layers, layouts, block placement, missing references, and fonts. Reopen a copy after saving to detect serialization problems early. Keep the original file available for comparison."},{"title":"Geometry is missing or distorted","chapter":"Troubleshooting and Recovery","url":"troubleshooting-recovery.html#geometry-is-missing-or-distorted","text":"Geometry is missing or distorted Try Zoom Extents, enable relevant layers, switch to wireframe, and inspect block scale, array settings, clipping boundaries, object normals, and coordinates far from the origin."},{"title":"Rendering is incomplete","chapter":"Troubleshooting and Recovery","url":"troubleshooting-recovery.html#rendering-is-incomplete","text":"Rendering is incomplete Reduce unusually large images or material textures, simplify extreme hatch or array inputs, and compare another visual style. Record the graphics backend and the smallest drawing that reproduces the issue."},{"title":"Undo did not restore the expected state","chapter":"Troubleshooting and Recovery","url":"troubleshooting-recovery.html#undo-did-not-restore-the-expected-state","text":"Undo did not restore the expected state Stop editing and save a diagnostic copy. Try the next adjacent undo entry only when you understand what it represents. Compare the result with autosave or backup data before overwriting the original drawing."},{"title":"Report a reproducible problem","chapter":"Troubleshooting and Recovery","url":"troubleshooting-recovery.html#report-a-reproducible-problem","text":"Report a reproducible problem Include the OpenCAD Studio version, platform, native or web build, exact steps, expected and observed results, and a minimal sanitized drawing when possible. Do not publish confidential drawings or external-reference paths."}];
